  <section id="predict" aria-labelledby="predict-h">
    <h2 id="predict-h">How Race Predictions Work</h2>
    <p>The calculator uses Peter Riegel&rsquo;s formula, T2 = T1 &times; (D2 &divide; D1)<sup>1.06</sup>. It works best between 5K and the half marathon and for runners trained for the target distance; marathon predictions from short races are often optimistic for newer runners.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="tips" aria-labelledby="tips-h">
    <h2 id="tips-h">Pacing Tips</h2>
    <p>Start slightly slower than goal pace for the first mile or kilometer, settle into an even effort, and save any surge for the final stretch; even or negative splits usually give the best times. For easy runs, use heart rate rather than pace: see the <a href="/heart-rate-zone-calculator/">heart rate zone calculator</a>. To check fitness, estimate your <a href="/vo2-max-calculator/">VO2 max</a> from a race result.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Running Pace FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is a good running pace?</h3></summary><p>For recreational runners, 9 to 12 minutes per mile is common for easy runs. A 5K pace under 8 minutes per mile is quick for a casual runner; elite marathoners average under 5 minutes per mile.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How do I calculate my pace per mile?</h3></summary><p>Divide your total time in minutes by the distance in miles. 30 minutes for 3 miles is 10 minutes per mile.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>What is a 10 minute mile in km?</h3></summary><p>A 10:00 per mile pace is 6:13 per km, or 6 mph (9.7 km/h).</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How long does it take to run a 5K?</h3></summary><p>Most recreational runners finish a 5K in 25 to 40 minutes. At 9:00 per mile it takes about 28 minutes; at 12:00 per mile about 37 minutes.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How accurate are race time predictions?</h3></summary><p>Within a few percent for distances close to the race you entered, if you train for the new distance. Predictions to the marathon from a 5K tend to be too fast for runners who do few long runs.</p></details>
  </section>

